{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790009449,"nonce":"ZX0cyxdR63SopX2LkkRKRzvEJks=","message":"Decided 2026-09-21: (a), a LexoRank-style fractional index.\n\nShape: rank is a lexicographic string key. Inserting between two neighbours computes a key strictly between theirs (midpoint in base-36/62, extending length when there is no room), so a drag touches exactly one issue — one SetField op, no renumbering, and no multi-entity commit.\n\nThe property that makes it conflict-free: sort by (rank, entity.Id), never by rank alone. Two people dragging different cards to the same slot independently compute the same midpoint key, and the tie then breaks deterministically by id — so both drags survive, every clone agrees on the resulting order, and nobody's drag silently disappears. That is the whole reason this is not a plain ordinal.\n\nCosts, accepted: keys grow as repeated inserts subdivide the same gap, so a rebalance pass renumbers a list when keys get too long. Rebalancing IS a multi-issue rewrite (N commits, eventually consistent), but it is rare and order-preserving, so a half-applied rebalance still reads correctly — unlike a half-applied renumber under option (b), which would scramble the order.\n\nImplementation can wait for f32ea71; the encoding lands with 5b09ee1 so the value format does not have to change later.","files":null}]}